<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="sparc:BE:32:default">
<!--
    Issue #796: on SPARC a function returning one int printed as a long long
    whose high word is its second argument when the int it returns is a
    literal zero.

        keep_zero:  long long keep_zero(uint4 *a0,uint4 a1)
                    { *a0 = a1; return (uint8)a1 << 0x20; }
        same:       if (a0 == a1) return 1; return (uint8)a1 << 0x20;

    restore copies %i1 back into %o1 on every return, so %o1 passes ancestor
    realism and return recovery joins %o0:%o1, first register low.  The
    uncomputed-half repair drops the %o1 half when it is the untouched
    argument, but the rule pool had already folded PIECE(a1, 0) into
    ZEXT(a1) << 32, which the repair did not read.  A nonzero literal stays
    a PIECE and was already repaired (keep_five).  The repair now also reads
    the folded form, for a pair joined first register low on an ABI that
    returns the first register high, where that form is never a 64-bit value
    the function returns.

    Stage: P4 calls and prototypes (the uncomputed half of a recovered return
    pair, ActionOutputPrototype).
    No option: a strict fix (the repair reads the form the rules give its pair).
    Source (clang 14 -fintegrated-as -fno-pic, sparc-linux-gnu):
        at 0x10000 (-O2):
        int keep_zero(int *p, int b) { *p = b; return 0; }
        unsigned same(unsigned a, unsigned b) { return a == b; }
        int flag_zero(int *p, int b) { if (b) *p = b; return 0; }
        int keep_five(int *p, int b) { *p = b; return 5; }
        long long ll_const(void) { return 0x100000002LL; }
        unsigned long long ull_arg(unsigned a) { return a; }
        long long ll_mul(int a, int b) { return (long long)a * b; }
        at 0x20000 (-O0): keep_zero, flag_zero and ull_arg as above.
    Assert #1: no function returns its second argument shifted into the high
    word.  Asserts #2-#4: keep_zero, same and flag_zero return an int, at -O2
    and -O0, and return 0 on the path that returns 0.  Assert #5: keep_five
    keeps its literal.  Assert #6: the 64-bit returns keep their width.
-->
